{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1522669-abilities-specialties-well-skilled-and-how-these-combine-in-various-ways","id":1522669,"title":"Abilities, Specialties, Well-Skilled, and how these combine in various ways","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-09-16T06:37:51-07:00","id":1522669,"title":"Abilities, Specialties, Well-Skilled, and how these combine in various ways","content":"In preparation for my group\u2019s character creation (mostly happened today, although each character still has a few details to work through\u2026 except my wife\u2019s character, who basically has a few details on paper and 90% to work out, but she lives here so we can work through it over dinner and such) I read a few bits of the rulebook a bit more closely than I had recently, paying close attention to certain details, and I noticed something.<br /> <br /> There are certain abilities that work a bit differently to others in regards to specialties and their overall scope of use. Most abilities you just buy dots in, and when you get to 4 dots you choose a specialty. But then there is a second groups of abilities that allow a character to have multiple specialties, and the (optional) Well-Skilled Craftsman rule applies. This second group includes: Art, Athletics, Crafts, Firearms, Martial Arts, Melee, Academics, Esoterica, Lore, Politics, Science, and the optional abilities categories of Professional Skills, Expert Knowledges, and Hobby Talents.<br /> <br /> And when I had read the rules before, I had simply assumed that this second group of abilities all work in pretty much the same way, but when I read the abilities descriptions more carefully just recently, I noticed discrepancies. (And I\u2019m not sure those discrepancies are bad, because I had been a bit dissatisfied with these abilities overall, and the \u201cdiscrepancies\u201d actually make a bit happier.)<br /> <br /> Under Craft, the book says: \u201cWhen purchasing this Skill, you must define a specialty for your character even if you don't have the usual four dots in the Trait; this specialty defines a type of Craft you pursue, and you get the bonus once you achieve at least four dots in that field. If you want your character to have several Crafts, they should be purchased separately. A mechanic who's also good with ceramics, woodworking, and home repair, for example, would have several different Craft specialties to reflect her ability with each one.\u201d<br /> <br /> But under Firearms, the book says, \u201cWith the proper training and experience, however, specialties for the Firearms Skill can cover heavy weapons: machine guns, bazookas, rocket launchers, and so on; see The Well-Skilled Craftsman, above.\u201d There\u2019s no mention there of choosing a specialty prior to achieving four dots. And while I\u2019ve only copied part of the description, it\u2019s just because the rest isn\u2019t remotely relevant. Check out the full description of Firearms in the book yourself.<br /> <br /> For Athletics, the description says: \u201cLike Art, Athletics has many different specialties \u2013 running, climbing, swimming, and so forth. And like Art, Crafts, and other broad categories, you can buy specialties to reflect different athletic pursuits. (The Well-Skilled Craftsman option applies here as well.)\u201d So this references Crafts, which has the rule about choosing a specialty before 4 dots, but does not itself mention choosing a specialty before achieving the fourth dot of Athletics. And Athletics has applications that do not seem to be restricted by specialty, such as its use for dodging in combat.<br /> <br /> Examining other abilities in the category, each references The Well-Skilled Craftsman rule, but the language concerning the application of specialties, and when the first specialty is chosen, varies between skills.<br /> <br /> And I\u2019m thinking that my assumption that these skills should be treated the same was a mistake. Because I\u2019m finding that I\u2019m pretty comfortable with the idea that some of these skills would work better if treated a bit differently to others.<br /> <br /> There is some ambiguity in the precise wording of the descriptions of some of these skills, but by my current reading it seems to me that Art, Crafts, Academics, and Science require a character to choose a specialty regardless of the number of dots they have, and cannot be used for tasks covered by other specialties (although obviously there will be overlap sometimes). Athletics, Firearms, Martial Arts, Melee, and Politics do not ask the player to choose a specialty before 4 dots, but allow the character to obtain the benefits associated with specialties by buying additional specialties once the 4th dot (and the free specialty that comes with it) has been achieved. And Esoterica and Lore are vague enough in their wording that I don\u2019t yet know what to do with them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-16T11:30:48-07:00","id":1522678,"title":"","content":"The side bar on Specialties, seen on page 274, makes it clear that you may choose a specialty for any ability you like in order to pinpoint an area of expertise. The speciality however does not grant you the 10s counting as double successes until you reach the 4th dot. It then states that certain abilities indeed require you to take a speciality immediately, like Crafts. For some abilities, this is because they are so broad that they cannot be represented so simply in general terms, this includes things such as Arts, Academics, and Crafts, as you have pointed out.<br /> Animal Kinship states the need for a speciality when it comes to training a specific animal, Lore is another one which requires you to choose a specialty in order to specify which splat you have specific information in. Similarly, Esoterica very much relies on you picking a specific speciality that fits your practice in order to make use of it, part of this is because Esoterica is created as an amalgam of main disparate abilities which were introduced over the many source books that came out, with abilities such as yoga and Enochian amongst them. <br /> In fact, the specific boon that using Esoterica allows would simply be far too powerful if it was liberally applied to all forms of mystic practice, in the same way that having 3 dots in Lore shouldn't give you insight on Vampires, Technocrats, Wraiths, and Werewolves indiscriminately.<br /> To support that point, Book of Secrets FAQ section specifically states that part of the process for a mage to develop a new practice is to purchase a new Esoterica Specialty BoS Pg174<br /> Indeed, Area Knowledge is also implied to require one set out a specialty for a specific area upon taking it, with an example on page 469 of having Area Knowledge of the digital web.<br /> <br /> I would therefor go ahead and say all abilities which reference the Well Skilled Craftsman optional rule intend for the dots you take in the ability to be towards a specified area within that ability, and yes I would say this includes Athletics, Martial Arts (which does specify you much choose a hard, soft, mixed, or weapon forms), and Politics. Firearms, and Melee.<br /> <br /> Regarding Firearms. It brings up heavy weapons in specific because are not usable unless you have the specialty for heavy weapons, this includes all Military Weapons as depicted on page 453 of the core M20. This is also true with operating Military vehicles with the correct Drive specialty in page 461, and Pilot for aircrafts, pg 462<br /> <br /> It should also be noted that a number of abilities provided by the Book of Secrets supply ranks for what may otherwise be considered as specialties for another, and indeed the book does say this is how the can be used. This includes Blind Fighting as an option for Brawl, Climbing and Swimming, as an option for Athletics, Jury-Rigging as an option for Technology, Networking as an option for Politics, Gunsmith for crafts...etc. They enable one to either choose them as their own separate abilities, or to have them as a specialty in one of the primary abilities and let said speciality unlock a few options or provide some parameters on to how they could be used.<br /> <br /> To sum up. Specialties are not just for the 4th rank. They are there to specify which aspect of the ability your mage focuses on, and the area they specialise on is supposed to be what is emphasized whenever they try to use the ability for that purpose. Some specialites will unlock access to certain things such as tools, maneuvers, mechanics, etc. But the rules are intentionally loose and uncomprehensive, more to give an idea of the sorts of things you as the ST can provide as a result of having said specialist training/knowledge<br /> Speak","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-16T19:06:59-07:00","id":1522683,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1522669-abilities-specialties-well-skilled-and-how-these-combine-in-various-ways?p=1522678#post1522678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The side bar on Specialties, seen on page 274, makes it clear that you may choose a specialty for any ability you like in order to pinpoint an area of expertise.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u201cAnd even if you don't start off with 4 dots or more in a given Trait, you can still choose a specialty for it; certain Traits \u2013 like Crafts \u2013 actually require you to choose a particular specialty. That specialty won't grant the additional-success bonus until you reach a rating of 4, but it can flesh out your character by showing areas of expertise that will eventually help your mage excel in future tasks.\u201d So the sidebar allows players to choose a specialty for any trait, but it says nothing about that unlocking applications of that trait that require a specialty. Rules requiring the possession of a specialty to perform particular tasks within the purview of the trait are always in the trait description, or perhaps elsewhere in the rules.<br /> <br /> So the issue is not whether a character can have a specialty before having 4 dots in an ability, but about whether the single specialty that the character can choose while having less than 4 dots unlocks new applications of the ability. I have found no general rule about that directly stated by the rulebook.<br /> <br /> And the reason I am happier after having had this realisation, and starting to consider that an ability being in the list in the sidebar Optional Rule: The Well-Skilled Craftsman is not enough to necessarily require a field of focus defined by a specialty, is because I am unwilling to say that a character with 3 dots in Athletics can only swim if they choose the Swimming specialty, and only climb if they choose the Climbing specialty; and thus no character can have the ability to both swim and climb until they first achieve 4 dots in Athletics. 4 dots represents a professional level of skill. Seriously, I\u2019m no pro athlete - I am under medical instruction to avoid running for the rest of my life to avoid possibly permanent physical disability in other areas - but even I can both climb and swim, and have been able to since I was a toddler. It\u2019s just not reasonable to require 4 dots to unlock such combinations of abilities.<br /> <br /> And by my reading of the rules, it is Arts, Crafts, Academics, and Science that ask the player to use a specialty to unlock a particular area of expertise even without having 4 dots. Athletics, Firearms, Martial Arts, Melee, and Politics do not. Esoterica and Lore were simply vague enough in their wording that I wanted (and still want) to read through them more carefully, although yes, Saikou, I am currently inclined to count them among the first group, rather than the second.<br /> <br /> And I have only gone over the abilities referenced by The Well-Skilled Craftsman sidebar looking for information about this. I haven\u2019t hunted for all references to The Well-Skilled Craftsman in all abilities. So yes, there are some I haven\u2019t included in either list.<br /> <br /> Some specific cases\u2026<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1522669-abilities-specialties-well-skilled-and-how-these-combine-in-various-ways?p=1522678#post1522678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regarding Firearms. It brings up heavy weapons in specific because are not usable unless you have the specialty for heavy weapons, this includes all Military Weapons as depicted on page 453 of the core M20.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is unclear on page 453 whether the Heavy Weapons specialty requirement is for using the military weapons, or is just a requirement for legal ownership of those weapons. There is nothing anywhere suggesting that a character with the Handguns specialty cannot use a rifle (or vice versa), only the special-case footnote concerning Heavy Weapons specialty that you mention on page 453.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1522669-abilities-specialties-well-skilled-and-how-these-combine-in-various-ways?p=1522678#post1522678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is also true with operating Military vehicles with the correct Drive specialty in page 461, and Pilot for aircrafts, pg 462</div> </div> </div> </div>Drive is neither referenced by The Well-Skilled Craftsman rule, nor references it. Given the option to use a specialty to allow riding a motorcycle, it\u2019s another case where the written rules become simply unreasonable. It is not hard to conceive of a character who first learned to ride a motorcycle (2 dots in drive and Motorcycle specialty - even learning to ride a motorcycle without learning to also drive a car is something some real-world people do) and then learned to drive a tank.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11151,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","user":"travisjhall","date":". 09-16-2023, 10:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-17T01:06:32-07:00","id":1522689,"title":"","content":"It really depends on how rigid you want to be with it.<br /> <br /> For my part, I treat specialities more as a focus than as a binary.<br /> <br /> if a mage has Science (Biology), I will allow rolls on things that are tangental to Biology, such as biochemistry, ecology, pharmacology, but perhaps at an increased difficulty to depict the departure from their focus.<br /> <br /> similarly, someone who has trained at climbing would be able to use athletics at standard diff for anything that resembles the skills used in climbing, such as hanging off of ledges, pulling your own weight, or staying on the side of a sheer drop for an extended period of time, but things that Are otherwise adjacent, such as treading water, lifting something heavy, jumping a gap, etc, I might have an increased difficulty for that to reflect the lack of experience with such feats.<br /> <br /> The system does allow for the ST to assign difficulty as they think makes the most sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/959615-chronicle-report-keepers-of-the-wyck\" target=\"_blank\">Keepers of the Wyck: A Chronicle I'm running</a> FINALE: Chapter 39: Green Fairy","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12193-saikou","name":"Saikou","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12193_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-17T02:58:00-07:00","id":1522692,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saikou</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1522669-abilities-specialties-well-skilled-and-how-these-combine-in-various-ways?p=1522689#post1522689\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It really depends on how rigid you want to be with it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Saikou, you\u2019re welcome to use whatever techniques you like to be a bit less rigid than the book. My point here is simply that a strict reading of the book is less rigid than it appears at first glance - even less rigid than a bit of flex on target numbers for specialty-adjacent use of an ability will allow.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11151-travisjhall","name":"travisjhall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-09-17T03:41:27-07:00","id":1522693,"title":"","content":"What if, regardless if you have 4-dots in a skill or not, if you put one extra dot in a skill, you can make it a specialty (I believe NWoD has something like that, but don't quote me). <br /> <br /> There's also Secondary-skills, from the Vampire's Player's Guide that could work for a specific-type of athletics. <br /> <br /> I did toy with the idea that if you play a Sleeper/Commoner, you can put a specialty, say, in melee, if your character-concept was a warrior, but, I like Ars Magica's approach, which was, it's a game focused on mages and magic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}